Z||000000||maryjane||05-17-2001||08:53 PM||||Can someone explain how (or where) the air from the rad cooling fan exits the front compartment on an 84? I've looked all over and can't figure it unless it just goes down. seems like it would be trapped there. thanks<BR>Don & Jane<BR>Sorry, meant to post this in tech section but don't know how to move it.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 05-17-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||05-17-2001||08:55 PM||||Yes it just kinda sits there. That is why folks go with "hood vents" to aid in cooling.||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||05-17-2001||10:52 PM||||I think the 84 fascia has the largest opening for the radiator though, so more air can be rammed into the radiator than some year Fieros, but it isnt very efficient for any model just because the air must also try to find a way out.  I think the GT fascia with a front plate on it is the worse.<P>It also dosent help if you have AC, since the AC cooler is in front of the radiator, so it partially blocks the flow of air.<P>The upside to having AC is that you can turn on the fan if you are sitting in traffic and you see your temp start to rise.  All you need to do is turn on the AC, and if the car is moving at less than 35 mph the front fan will turn on, sucking air through the radiators.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
